An IELTS candidate reads the app in English and learns to hear words that differ in their first consonant ("pat" and "bat", "pin" and "bin"). Nothing is translated: words get short plain-English definitions that never contain the word, and sentence `translation` fields repeat the sentence. The lesson is about hearing a sound, which writing can't test: a written word gives its spelling away, so practice never asks about a sound or letter in writing ("The first sound in pat is ___" with options "p", "t", "b" fails). Practice tests the contrast through meaning instead: a sentence where only one word of the pair fits ("He hit the ball with a ___": "bat", with "pat" as a wrong option). Each sentence uses one word of a pair, and its distractors include the other word of that pair, so hearing the sentence and building it tests the sound. Tips say how to make each sound, without claims about "your language". There is no dialogue: its written lines would show the word a learner should tell apart by ear.